---
import GlossaryEntry from '../../components/GlossaryEntry.astro';
---

<GlossaryEntry
  term="Agent Attestation"
  slug="agent-attestation"
  title="What Is Agent Attestation? Definition, Roles, and Verifiability | Kyde Glossary"
  description="Agent attestation is signed, verifiable proof that an AI agent operated within its mandate. The definition, the three roles that make it trustworthy, and why the witness must be independent."
  definition="Agent attestation is the practice of producing signed, verifiable proof that an AI agent operated within its mandate over a defined period: which policies applied, what evidence was recorded, who signed off. Third parties such as auditors, insurers, or enterprise customers can verify the attestation without access to the underlying raw data."
  who="Organizations that need to demonstrate agent trustworthiness to someone outside the building: an auditor preparing a compliance opinion, an insurer pricing coverage, a procurement team evaluating an agent-powered vendor, a partner deciding whether your agent may touch their API. Attestation turns internal governance into an artifact others can rely on."
  related={[
    { label: 'AI Agent Audit Trail', href: '/glossary/ai-agent-audit-trail' },
    { label: 'Agent Autonomy Levels', href: '/glossary/agent-autonomy-levels' },
    { label: 'AI Agent Trust Score', href: '/glossary/ai-agent-trust-score' },
    { label: 'AI Agent Insurance Guide', href: '/ai-agent-insurance' },
  ]}
>
  <div>
    <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-ink-0 uppercase tracking-wide mb-4">What makes an attestation trustworthy</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-1 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-4">
      An attestation is only as credible as the record behind it and the independence of whoever produced that record. Self-attestation from editable logs is marketing. Trustworthy attestation requires a separation of roles, the same one every serious audit regime uses: the party operating the system, the party recording its behavior, and the party verifying the claims cannot be the same entity with the same incentives.
    </p>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-1 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
      The structural rule that follows: the witness, whoever records the evidence, must not profit when the agent is granted more autonomy. A model vendor attesting its own agents fails that test by construction. Player, referee, and scorekeeper have to be different people.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="border border-line-0 bg-bg-1 p-8 max-w-3xl overflow-x-auto">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-2 mb-5">Data point · the three roles</p>
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-line-0 text-ink-1">
        <tr>
          <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-0">Operator</td>
          <td class="py-3">Runs the agents, benefits from their autonomy. Cannot credibly attest itself.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-0">Witness</td>
          <td class="py-3">Records every action into a tamper-evident ledger. Must be structurally independent of agent and model vendors.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-0">Verifier</td>
          <td class="py-3">Checks the evidence and signs the attestation: auditor, insurer, or regulator. Needs hash proofs, not raw data.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="text-xs text-ink-2 mt-4">Verification without raw data access works through hash references: the artifact carries checksums of the record, not its contents. Sovereignty-compatible by design.</p>
  </div>
</GlossaryEntry>
